g in California, the eventual nominees, and shifts in the national environment could move the margin in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "California remains one of the most Democratic-leaning states in the country, and the 2028 presidential race there starts with that structural advantage. The incumbent president is term-limited, so neither party benefits from incumbency, and no candidate has yet built a general-election operation in the state. Polling so far has focused on the Democratic primary, where Newsom, Harris, and others compete for the nomination, rather than on the general election against the Republican ticket.\n\nWith no California general-election surveys or expert ratings available, the forecast rests on the state's D+12 partisan lean. That points to a Democratic win in the Likely range, though the margin of uncertainty is wide. A strong Republican nominee, a significant third-party bid, or an unusually favorable national environment could narrow the gap, but recent history suggests California stays comfortably blue in presidential years.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9157, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8615, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8622, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8596, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8693, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8671, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8635, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8619, "margin": 12.0}]}